TY  - JOUR
TI  - Cooptive Corporate Actor Networks: A Reconsideration of Interlocking Directorates Involving American Manufacturing
AU  - Burt, Ronald S.
AB  - [The representation of establishments in diverse sectors of the economy on the board of directors for a given corporation via three types of cooptive relations - ownership, direct interlocking with other firms, and indirect interlocking with other firms through financial institutions - is defined as the corporation's cooptive corporate actor network. Analysis of such a network is demonstrated to replicate basic findings on interlocking directorates that have been obtained using more traditional methodology and to extend those findings in a substantively meaningful way. Network range (defined as the diversity of establishments represented on a corporate board: many establishments from many sectors) is shown to be greatest for large firms controlled by diffuse interest groups. The three types of cooptive relations are shown to be used as complements rather than alternatives; sectors represented via one type of relation tend to be represented via the other two types. In addition to representing sectors in which it owns establishments, a firm tends to develop and coordinate cooptive relations with sectors in which it does not own establishments. As is the case for network range, this coordination of different types of cooptive relations (network multiplexity) is shown to be greatest for large firms controlled by diffuse interest groups. I close with conclusions regarding the propriety of the corporation as the unit of analysis in research describing the directorate as a cooptive device.]
